/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Science: how the world really works */
WB.L("magnetism", "science", "beginner", "Magnetism: the invisible pull",
  "A fridge magnet, a compass needle and the southern lights are all showing you the same hidden force at work.",
  [
    ["A rock that points north", "People knew magnetic rocks for thousands of years before anyone could explain them.",
      "Long before anyone understood it, people noticed that certain dark rocks could pull iron towards them. These rocks, called lodestones, are made of a mineral called magnetite that has become naturally magnetised, possibly by lightning strikes. Ancient Greek and Chinese writers both described them. In China, lodestones were shaped into spoons that swung round to point in a fixed direction, and by around the eleventh century Chinese sailors were using magnetised needles to steer at sea. The compass later spread to the Arab world and Europe, where it helped make the great ocean voyages possible. For centuries, though, nobody could say why a sliver of iron should care which way north was. The answer turned out to lie deep inside atoms, and deep inside the Earth itself."],
    ["Where magnetism comes from", "Every electron behaves like a tiny magnet, and magnetism appears when those tiny magnets line up.",
      "Every atom contains electrons, and each electron behaves like a minuscule bar magnet with its own north and south pole. This comes from a quantum property physicists call spin, along with the way electrons move around the nucleus. In most materials, electrons pair up so that their magnetic effects point in opposite directions and cancel out. Wood, plastic, water and your own body are mostly like this, which is why a fridge magnet ignores them. In a few materials, however, some electrons are left unpaired, and their little magnetic fields can add together instead of cancelling. When enough of them point the same way, the whole object becomes magnetic. So a magnet is not doing anything mysterious: it is simply what happens when billions upon billions of tiny magnets agree on a direction."],
    ["Why only some metals stick", "Iron, nickel and cobalt contain magnetic domains that can be lined up, and heat can scramble them again.",
      "Try sticking a magnet to a soft drink can and it will slide off, because aluminium is barely magnetic. A steel food tin is a different story. Iron, nickel and cobalt are the classic magnetic metals, a behaviour called ferromagnetism. Inside them, atoms group into tiny regions called domains, each one already magnetised in its own direction. In an ordinary nail, the domains point every which way and cancel out. Bring a strong magnet near and the domains swing into line, so the nail itself becomes a magnet for a while. Permanent magnets are materials whose domains stay aligned. Heat works against all this order. Above a certain temperature, called the Curie point, which is about 770 degrees Celsius for iron, the jiggling of atoms scrambles the domains and the magnetism disappears."],
    ["Electricity and magnetism are one force", "In the 1800s scientists discovered that moving electric charges make magnetism, and moving magnets make electricity.",
      "In 1820, the Danish physicist Hans Christian Oersted noticed something odd during a lecture demonstration: a compass needle twitched whenever he switched on an electric current in a nearby wire. Electricity, it seemed, could create magnetism. About a decade later, in 1831, Michael Faraday in London showed the reverse. Moving a magnet near a coil of wire made a current flow, an effect called electromagnetic induction. In the 1860s, the Scottish physicist James Clerk Maxwell wrote down a set of equations showing that electricity and magnetism are two faces of a single force, electromagnetism. His equations also predicted waves of electric and magnetic fields travelling at the speed of light. The conclusion was startling: light itself is an electromagnetic wave, and so are radio waves, microwaves and X-rays."],
    ["Magnets that run the modern world", "Generators, motors, speakers and medical scanners all rely on the link between magnets and electricity.",
      "Faraday's discovery quietly powers your life. Nearly every power station, whether it burns coal, uses falling water or harnesses wind, makes electricity the same basic way: something spins a magnet and a coil of wire relative to each other, and induction pushes a current through the coil. Electric motors run the trick backwards, using current and magnets to create a spinning force. There are motors in your fridge, washing machine, car windows and phone's vibrating buzzer. Speakers and headphones use a magnet and a coil to push a thin cone back and forth, turning an electrical signal into sound. Hospitals use enormously powerful magnets in MRI machines, which line up the hydrogen atoms in your body and then listen to the faint radio signals they give off, producing detailed images without X-rays."],
    ["Earth's magnetic shield", "Swirling molten iron deep underground makes Earth a giant magnet that protects us from the solar wind.",
      "The Earth behaves like a huge magnet, which is why compasses work. Its field is generated about 3,000 kilometres below your feet, in the outer core, where molten iron churns and flows. That moving, electrically conducting metal acts as a natural dynamo, keeping the field going. The field reaches far out into space and deflects most of the solar wind, a stream of charged particles pouring off the Sun. Some of those particles are funnelled towards the poles, where they collide with gases in the upper atmosphere and make them glow: in the Southern Hemisphere, that glow is the aurora australis, sometimes seen from Tasmania. The field is restless. The magnetic poles wander, and every so often the whole field flips. The last full reversal happened roughly 780,000 years ago."],
    ["Animals with an inner compass", "Many animals sense Earth's magnetic field, although exactly how they do it is still being worked out.",
      "Humans need a compass, but many animals seem to carry one inside them. Experiments have shown that migratory birds, sea turtles, salmon and even some insects use Earth's magnetic field to find their way. Young loggerhead turtles, for example, appear to read the field's strength and angle to stay on course across thousands of kilometres of open ocean. How they actually sense it is one of biology's lingering puzzles. One leading idea is that light-sensitive proteins in birds' eyes react to magnetism, perhaps letting them 'see' the field as a faint pattern laid over their vision. Another is that tiny grains of magnetite in some animals' bodies act like compass needles. Both may be true in different species. Either way, the force holding a shopping list to your fridge steers animals around the planet."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What are naturally magnetic rocks called?", ["Lodestones", "Flintstones", "Moonstones", "Bloodstones"], 0, "Lodestones are pieces of the mineral magnetite that have become naturally magnetised."],
    [2, "What happens to iron above its Curie point?", ["It becomes more strongly magnetic", "It loses its magnetism as heat scrambles its domains", "It turns into aluminium", "It starts producing electricity"], 1, "Above about 770 degrees Celsius, atomic jiggling scrambles iron's magnetic domains."],
    [3, "What did Maxwell's equations reveal about light?", ["It is made of tiny magnets", "It travels slower than sound", "It is an electromagnetic wave", "It cannot pass through space"], 2, "Maxwell showed that waves of electric and magnetic fields travel at the speed of light, so light is electromagnetic."],
    [5, "What generates Earth's magnetic field?", ["Iron ore in the crust", "The Moon's gravity", "The solar wind", "Moving molten iron in the outer core"], 3, "Churning liquid iron in the outer core acts as a dynamo that sustains the field."],
    [6, "Which statement about animal magnetic sense is accurate?", ["Only birds can sense magnetism", "Scientists are still working out exactly how animals sense the field", "It was disproved in the 1990s", "It only works at night"], 1, "Many animals use the field, but the mechanism is still an open research question."],
  ]);

WB.L("sound", "science", "beginner", "How sound travels",
  "Every voice, song and thunderclap is just air being squeezed and stretched, and the physics explains everything from echoes to sonic booms.",
  [
    ["A wave you can't see", "Sound is a travelling ripple of pressure, not a stream of air rushing from the source to your ear.",
      "Clap your hands and you shove the air between them outwards. Those air molecules bump into their neighbours, which bump into theirs, and a ripple of squashed and stretched air spreads out in every direction. That travelling ripple is sound. It is a pressure wave, and the air itself barely moves: each molecule just nudges back and forth and passes the push along, a bit like a ripple running down a slinky. When the wave reaches you, it pushes and pulls on your eardrum, which vibrates in step. Tiny bones in your middle ear pass that vibration to the fluid filled cochlea, where thousands of hair cells turn it into nerve signals. Your brain then does the remarkable work of turning those signals into a friend's laugh or a favourite song."],
    ["The speed of sound", "Sound travels about 343 metres per second in air, and much faster through water and solids.",
      "In air at around 20 degrees Celsius, sound travels about 343 metres every second, roughly a kilometre in three seconds. Light is almost a million times faster, which is why you see lightning before you hear the thunder. You can use this to estimate how far away a storm is: count the seconds between the flash and the rumble, then divide by three to get the distance in kilometres. Sound moves faster in warm air than cold air, and much faster through liquids and solids, where particles are packed closely and pass the push along more quickly. In seawater it travels at roughly 1,500 metres per second, and in steel several times faster again. That is why you can hear an approaching train through the rails before you hear it through the air."],
    ["Pitch and frequency", "How high or low a sound seems depends on how many times per second the wave vibrates.",
      "The number of pressure waves that pass each second is called the frequency, measured in hertz. A deep note vibrates slowly and a high note vibrates quickly. The A above middle C, which orchestras often tune to, is usually set at 440 hertz, meaning 440 vibrations every second. Young people can typically hear from about 20 hertz up to about 20,000 hertz. The top of that range shrinks with age, which is why some teenagers can hear a high pitched whine from electronics that their parents cannot. Below our range lies infrasound, used by elephants to communicate over long distances. Above it lies ultrasound, which dogs, bats and dolphins can hear. Musical instruments sound different even on the same note because each adds its own mix of higher frequencies, called overtones."],
    ["Loudness and decibels", "The decibel scale is logarithmic, so small increases in the number mean big jumps in sound energy.",
      "Loudness depends on how strongly the wave squeezes the air, and we measure it in decibels. The scale is logarithmic: every increase of 10 decibels means ten times more sound energy, and our ears hear it as roughly twice as loud. A quiet library might sit around 30 to 40 decibels, normal conversation around 60, and a loud concert can reach well over 100. That means a concert carries many millions of times more sound energy than a whisper. Your hearing is precious and hard to repair. Long or repeated exposure to sounds above roughly 85 decibels can permanently damage the delicate hair cells in your inner ear. This is general information rather than medical advice, so if you notice ringing or muffled hearing, healthdirect is a good place to start."],
    ["Echoes and seeing with sound", "Bats, dolphins, ships and hospital scanners all use returning echoes to build a picture of the world.",
      "Shout across a valley and the sound bounces off the far side and returns as an echo. Some animals have turned this into a superpower. Many bats send out rapid, high pitched calls and listen to the echoes, judging the distance, size and even texture of insects in total darkness. Dolphins and toothed whales do something similar underwater with clicks. This skill is called echolocation. Humans copied the idea with sonar, which ships and submarines use to map the seafloor and detect objects beneath the waves. Medical ultrasound works on the same principle. A probe sends very high frequency sound into the body and listens for echoes bouncing off organs and tissues, which a computer turns into an image. It is how many parents first see their baby on a screen."],
    ["The Doppler effect", "A sound's pitch rises as its source approaches you and falls as it moves away.",
      "Stand beside a road as an ambulance races past and you will hear its siren drop in pitch the moment it passes. The siren itself has not changed. As the ambulance comes towards you, each new sound wave is sent out from a little closer than the last, so the waves bunch up and arrive more often, giving a higher pitch. As it moves away, the waves are stretched out and the pitch falls. This is called the Doppler effect, after the Austrian physicist Christian Doppler, who described it in 1842. It works for any kind of wave. Police radar uses it with radio waves to measure a car's speed, weather radar uses it to track wind inside storms, and astronomers use it with light to tell how fast distant galaxies are moving away."],
    ["Silence in space and the sound barrier", "Sound needs something to travel through, and anything that outruns it creates a sonic boom.",
      "Science fiction films are full of roaring explosions in space, but real space would be silent. Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle, and the near vacuum between planets has almost no particles to carry it. Astronauts on a spacewalk talk to each other by radio, which uses electromagnetic waves that need no medium. Back on Earth, something interesting happens when an object travels faster than sound. A jet flying at supersonic speed outruns its own pressure waves, which pile up into a cone shaped shock wave. When that shock wave sweeps over you, you hear a sudden sonic boom. The first officially confirmed supersonic flight in level flight was made by American pilot Chuck Yeager in the Bell X-1 rocket plane in 1947. A cracking whip makes a tiny sonic boom too."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What actually travels from a sound source to your ear?", ["A stream of air rushing towards you", "A pressure wave passed between molecules", "Tiny particles of sound", "Light that your ears detect"], 1, "Air molecules only nudge back and forth, passing along a wave of pressure."],
    [1, "Roughly how far away is a storm if thunder arrives six seconds after the lightning?", ["About two kilometres", "About six kilometres", "About 600 metres", "About twenty kilometres"], 0, "Sound travels roughly a kilometre every three seconds, so six seconds means about two kilometres."],
    [3, "An increase of 10 decibels means about how much more sound energy?", ["Twice as much", "Ten per cent more", "Ten times as much", "A hundred times as much"], 2, "The decibel scale is logarithmic: each 10 decibel step is ten times more energy."],
    [4, "What do bats use echolocation for?", ["Keeping warm", "Finding and judging objects in the dark", "Attracting mates with song", "Measuring air pressure"], 1, "Bats listen to the echoes of their calls to locate insects and obstacles in darkness."],
    [5, "Why does an ambulance siren drop in pitch as it passes?", ["The driver changes the siren setting", "Sound slows down after passing you", "Your ears get tired", "The waves are stretched out as it moves away"], 3, "This is the Doppler effect: moving away stretches the waves, lowering the pitch."],
  ]);

WB.L("photosynthesis", "science", "beginner", "Photosynthesis: how plants eat sunlight",
  "A towering gum tree is built mostly from thin air, and the process that does it also made the oxygen you are breathing.",
  [
    ["Where a tree's weight comes from", "A tree's mass comes mostly from carbon dioxide in the air, not from the soil.",
      "In the 1600s, the Flemish scientist Jan Baptist van Helmont ran a famous experiment. He planted a small willow in a pot of carefully weighed, dried soil and gave it nothing but water for five years. The willow gained a great deal of weight, yet when he dried and weighed the soil again, it had barely changed. Van Helmont concluded that the tree was made from water. He was partly right, but he missed the most surprising ingredient. Most of the dry mass of a plant is carbon, and that carbon comes from carbon dioxide drawn out of the air. A huge gum tree is, in a very real sense, built from thin air, stitched together with water and powered by sunlight. The process that does this is called photosynthesis."],
    ["The basic recipe", "Plants use light energy to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugar, releasing oxygen as a by-product.",
      "Photosynthesis takes three simple ingredients and makes something far more valuable. A plant draws water up from its roots and takes in carbon dioxide through tiny pores in its leaves called stomata. Using the energy in sunlight, it rearranges these molecules into glucose, a sugar, and releases oxygen into the air. In chemical shorthand, six molecules of carbon dioxide plus six of water become one molecule of glucose and six of oxygen. The glucose is the plant's food and building material. It can be burned for energy, stored as starch, or linked into long chains of cellulose to make tough cell walls and wood. Almost every food you eat traces back to this reaction, whether you eat the plant directly or eat an animal that did."],
    ["Why leaves are green", "Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green, which is the colour we see.",
      "The green colour of leaves comes from a pigment called chlorophyll, packed inside tiny structures called chloroplasts. Chlorophyll is very good at absorbing red and blue light, which it uses to power photosynthesis. It is poor at absorbing green light, so much of that green bounces back towards our eyes. Leaves also contain yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids. In many deciduous trees, autumn reveals them. As days shorten, the tree stops making chlorophyll and breaks down what it has, and the hidden yellows and oranges show through. Red autumn colours are different: they often come from new pigments made in the leaf at that time of year. Most Australian native trees, including eucalypts, are evergreen, so the famous autumn displays here usually come from introduced species."],
    ["The oxygen comes from water", "Plants split water molecules, and the oxygen they release comes from that water, not from carbon dioxide.",
      "It seems natural to assume that the oxygen plants breathe out comes from the carbon dioxide they take in. After all, carbon dioxide contains oxygen. But experiments in the 1940s using a heavier, traceable form of oxygen showed something different. When scientists labelled the oxygen in water, the labelled oxygen appeared in the gas the plants released. When they labelled the carbon dioxide instead, it did not. The oxygen we breathe comes from water molecules that plants rip apart using the energy of light. This splitting of water is one of the hardest tricks in chemistry, and plants do it at ordinary temperatures, powered only by sunshine. Researchers are still trying to copy it efficiently in the lab, in the hope of making clean fuels such as hydrogen from sunlight and water."],
    ["Two stages, one factory", "Light reactions capture energy, and a second set of reactions uses it to build sugar from carbon dioxide.",
      "Photosynthesis happens in two linked stages inside the chloroplast. In the first, called the light dependent reactions, chlorophyll absorbs light and uses its energy to split water, release oxygen and charge up two energy carrying molecules, known as ATP and NADPH. Think of them as freshly charged batteries. In the second stage, often called the Calvin cycle after the American chemist Melvin Calvin, those batteries power the construction of sugar. Carbon dioxide from the air is grabbed by an enzyme called RuBisCO and fixed into an organic molecule, which is then reshaped step by step into sugar. RuBisCO is slow and sometimes grabs oxygen by mistake, so plants make vast amounts of it. It is often described as probably the most abundant protein on Earth."],
    ["Plants built for heat and drought", "Some plants, such as sugarcane and cacti, use clever variations of photosynthesis to cope with heat and dryness.",
      "Every time a plant opens its stomata to take in carbon dioxide, precious water escapes. In hot, dry places, that is a real problem. Some plants have evolved ways around it. Sugarcane, maize and many tropical grasses use a method called C4 photosynthesis. They first capture carbon dioxide into a small four carbon molecule, then concentrate it around RuBisCO, which makes the process more efficient in heat and strong light. Australia's spinifex grasses, which cover huge areas of the arid inland, also use this approach. Cacti, pineapples and many succulents go further with a method called CAM. They open their stomata only at night, when it is cooler and less water is lost, store the carbon dioxide as an acid, and then use it for photosynthesis during the day with their pores shut tight."],
    ["How photosynthesis changed the planet", "Tiny microbes that learned to photosynthesise filled the air with oxygen and transformed Earth.",
      "Photosynthesis is far older than plants. It was first developed by microbes, and one group, the cyanobacteria, worked out how to split water and release oxygen. Over hundreds of millions of years, their oxygen built up, and around 2.4 billion years ago the atmosphere changed dramatically in what geologists call the Great Oxidation Event. Some of that early oxygen reacted with iron dissolved in the oceans, helping to form banded iron formations. Western Australia's Pilbara region holds spectacular examples, and they supply much of the iron ore the country exports. Today, tiny ocean plankton are thought to produce around half of the world's oxygen, with land plants providing much of the rest. Even the coal, oil and gas we burn are stored sunlight, captured by photosynthesis millions of years ago."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does most of a tree's dry mass come from?", ["The soil", "Carbon dioxide from the air", "Minerals in rainwater", "Sunlight turning into matter"], 1, "Most of a plant's dry mass is carbon taken from carbon dioxide in the air."],
    [2, "Why do most leaves look green?", ["Chlorophyll reflects green light rather than absorbing it", "Leaves contain green sugar", "Green light is the strongest in sunlight", "Carotenoids are green"], 0, "Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light well but reflects much of the green."],
    [3, "Where does the oxygen released by plants come from?", ["Carbon dioxide", "Glucose", "Soil minerals", "Water molecules split using light energy"], 3, "Labelling experiments showed the oxygen comes from water, not carbon dioxide."],
    [5, "What is special about CAM plants such as cacti?", ["They do not need light", "They take in oxygen instead of carbon dioxide", "They open their pores at night to save water", "They live only in water"], 2, "CAM plants collect carbon dioxide at night, when less water is lost, and keep their pores shut by day."],
    [6, "What was the Great Oxidation Event?", ["A huge volcanic eruption", "The build up of oxygen in the atmosphere about 2.4 billion years ago", "The first forest fire", "The rusting of the Moon"], 1, "Oxygen released by cyanobacteria built up in the air around 2.4 billion years ago."],
  ]);

WB.L("cells", "science", "beginner", "The cell: life's building block",
  "You are a walking city of tens of trillions of cells, each one a busy factory with its own power stations, post office and instruction library.",
  [
    ["A look through a slice of cork", "Cells were first seen in the 1600s, when early microscopes opened up a hidden world.",
      "In 1665, the English scientist Robert Hooke published a book called Micrographia, full of detailed drawings of what he saw through his microscope. Looking at a thin slice of cork, he saw rows of tiny empty boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms monks lived in, so he called them cells. What he was actually seeing were the walls of dead plant cells. A few years later, the Dutch draper Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, who ground his own remarkably powerful lenses, looked at pond water and scrapings from his own teeth. He was astonished to find them swarming with tiny moving creatures he called animalcules. These were bacteria and other single celled organisms, and no human had ever seen them before. A whole living world had been invisible all along."],
    ["Cell theory", "All living things are made of cells, and every cell comes from an existing cell.",
      "It took nearly two centuries for scientists to grasp how important cells are. In the late 1830s, the German scientists Matthias Schleiden and Theodor Schwann argued that all plants and all animals are built from cells. In the 1850s, the physician Rudolf Virchow popularised a crucial addition: every cell comes from another cell. Together, these ideas became cell theory, one of the foundations of biology. It sounds simple, but it overturned the old belief that life could spring spontaneously from mud or rotting meat. It also changed medicine. If the body is a community of cells, then disease can be understood as something going wrong in those cells. Virchow used this thinking to study conditions such as cancer, and modern medicine still works at this cellular level."],
    ["How many cells you are", "An adult human body contains roughly 30 trillion cells, most of them red blood cells.",
      "Counting the cells in a human body is surprisingly hard, but recent scientific estimates suggest an average adult has somewhere around 30 trillion of them. That is thousands of times more than the number of people on Earth. By far the most numerous are red blood cells, which make up most of the total by count. They are tiny discs with no nucleus, built to carry oxygen. Cells come in a dazzling range of shapes and sizes. Some nerve cells send fibres that can run from the base of your spine down to your foot, about a metre long. A human egg cell is one of the largest and can just about be seen with the naked eye. You also carry a similar number of bacteria, mostly in your gut."],
    ["The membrane: a smart border", "Every cell is wrapped in a thin, oily membrane that controls what gets in and out.",
      "Every cell is surrounded by a membrane, a flexible skin only a few billionths of a metre thick. It is made of two layers of fatty molecules called phospholipids. Each has a head that likes water and a tail that avoids it, so in a watery environment they naturally line up tail to tail, forming a double layer that keeps the inside in and the outside out. But a cell cannot be sealed shut, because it needs to take in food and get rid of waste. So the membrane is studded with proteins that act as gates, pumps and messengers. Some let only certain substances through, such as sugar or particular salts. Others act like doorbells, receiving signals from hormones or other cells and passing the message inside, so the cell knows how to respond."],
    ["Inside the factory", "Cells contain specialised parts that store instructions, build proteins and supply energy.",
      "A typical animal or plant cell is crowded with specialised parts called organelles. The nucleus is the control centre, holding the DNA that contains instructions for building proteins. Those instructions are copied and sent out into the cell, where ribosomes, tiny molecular machines, read them and assemble proteins one building block at a time. A folded network called the endoplasmic reticulum helps make and transport many proteins, and the Golgi apparatus packages and labels them for delivery, a bit like a post office. Mitochondria release energy from food, producing a molecule called ATP that powers almost everything the cell does. Plant cells also contain chloroplasts for photosynthesis and a stiff wall for support. Bacteria are simpler cells with no nucleus at all; their DNA simply floats inside them."],
    ["Mitochondria were once bacteria", "The power stations in your cells are descended from free living bacteria swallowed long ago.",
      "One of the most surprising ideas in biology is that parts of your cells were once separate living things. The theory, championed by the American biologist Lynn Margulis from the late 1960s, says that mitochondria began as free living bacteria. Long ago, more than a billion years back, one cell engulfed another but did not digest it. Instead the two formed a partnership: the guest supplied energy and the host supplied shelter and food. Over time, the guest became a permanent part of the cell. The evidence is strong. Mitochondria have their own small loop of DNA, similar to bacterial DNA, and they divide on their own, much as bacteria do. Chloroplasts in plants appear to have the same kind of origin, from captured cyanobacteria. You inherit your mitochondria from your mother."],
    ["Dividing, renewing and going wrong", "Cells constantly divide to grow and repair the body, and cancer happens when that control breaks down.",
      "You began as a single fertilised egg, and every one of your trillions of cells came from that first cell dividing again and again. Before a cell divides, it copies its DNA, then splits so each new cell gets a full set. This process continues throughout life. The lining of your gut is renewed every few days, and red blood cells last only about four months before being replaced. Most nerve cells in your brain, however, are thought to last most of your life. Division is tightly controlled by signals telling a cell when to grow, stop or die. Cancer is what happens when mutations break those controls, so cells keep dividing when they should not. Understanding the cell is why modern cancer treatments can target specific faults."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Robert Hooke choose the word 'cell'?", ["The boxes looked like small rooms monks lived in", "It meant 'alive' in Latin", "They looked like prison bars", "It was Leeuwenhoek's term"], 0, "The cork's tiny boxes reminded Hooke of monks' small rooms, called cells."],
    [1, "Which idea is part of cell theory?", ["Life can arise from rotting meat", "Only animals are made of cells", "Every cell comes from another cell", "Cells are made of atoms of life"], 2, "Cell theory says all living things are made of cells and every cell comes from an existing cell."],
    [2, "Which cells are the most numerous in the human body by count?", ["Nerve cells", "Skin cells", "Fat cells", "Red blood cells"], 3, "Red blood cells make up most of the body's cells by number."],
    [4, "What do ribosomes do?", ["Store DNA", "Build proteins from instructions", "Release energy from food", "Form the cell wall"], 1, "Ribosomes read copied instructions and assemble proteins."],
    [5, "What is a key piece of evidence that mitochondria were once bacteria?", ["They are green", "They are found only in plants", "They have their own DNA, similar to bacterial DNA", "They can live outside the body"], 2, "Mitochondria carry their own bacterial style DNA and divide independently."],
  ]);

WB.L("radioactivity", "science", "intermediate", "Radioactivity: the restless atom",
  "Some atoms are quietly falling apart all around you, and learning to understand them gave us carbon dating, cancer treatment and nuclear power.",
  [
    ["A photograph in a drawer", "Radioactivity was discovered by accident in 1896 when uranium fogged a photographic plate.",
      "In 1896, the French physicist Henri Becquerel was investigating whether uranium salts, after soaking up sunlight, gave off the newly discovered X-rays. His plan was to put the salts in the sun on top of photographic plates wrapped in black paper, then see if the plates darkened. A spell of cloudy Paris weather interrupted the experiment, so he tucked everything away in a drawer. When he developed the plates anyway, he found clear images of the uranium salts. No sunlight had been needed. The uranium was giving off some kind of invisible, penetrating radiation all on its own, all the time. Becquerel had stumbled on a property of matter nobody had imagined: some atoms are not stable at all, but are slowly and spontaneously breaking down."],
    ["Marie Curie and the new elements", "Marie Curie named radioactivity, discovered two new elements and became the first person to win two Nobel Prizes.",
      "Becquerel's discovery caught the attention of a young Polish born scientist in Paris, Marie Sklodowska Curie. Working with her husband Pierre, she measured the mysterious rays and found that their strength depended on the amount of uranium, not its chemical form, suggesting the effect came from inside the atom itself. She coined the term radioactivity. Processing tonnes of uranium ore by hand in a leaky shed, the Curies discovered two new, intensely radioactive elements in 1898: polonium, named after her homeland, and radium. Marie Curie shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics and won the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, making her the first person to win two. The dangers were not understood then. She died in 1934 of an illness linked to radiation exposure, and her notebooks are still radioactive."],
    ["Alpha, beta and gamma", "Unstable atoms release three main kinds of radiation, each with very different penetrating power.",
      "An atom is radioactive when its nucleus has an unstable mix of protons and neutrons. To settle down, it throws off energy or particles, a process called decay. In the early 1900s, the New Zealand born physicist Ernest Rutherford and others identified three main types. Alpha particles are clumps of two protons and two neutrons. They are heavy and slow, and a sheet of paper or the outer layer of your skin stops them, though they are harmful if breathed in or swallowed. Beta particles are fast electrons, stopped by a thin sheet of aluminium. Gamma rays are high energy electromagnetic waves, like extremely powerful X-rays, and need thick lead or concrete to block them. When an atom gives off an alpha or beta particle, it actually turns into a different element."],
    ["Half-life: predictable randomness", "No one can predict when a single atom will decay, but a large group decays at a perfectly regular rate.",
      "Radioactive decay is truly random. There is no way to predict when any particular unstable atom will break down. Yet in a large collection of atoms, the overall pattern is beautifully regular. The half-life is the time it takes for half of the atoms in a sample to decay. After one half-life, half remain; after two, a quarter; after three, an eighth. Half-lives range wildly, from tiny fractions of a second to billions of years. Uranium-238 has a half-life of about 4.5 billion years, roughly the age of the Earth. Carbon-14, which is constantly made in the atmosphere and absorbed by living things, has a half-life of about 5,730 years. Measuring how much is left in old bones, wood or charcoal lets scientists date them back roughly 50,000 years."],
    ["Radiation all around us", "We are exposed to low levels of natural background radiation every day, from rocks, space and even food.",
      "Radiation is not only found in power plants and laboratories. Everyone is exposed to a low level of natural background radiation all the time. Some comes from rocks and soil, which contain traces of uranium, thorium and potassium. Some comes from radon, a radioactive gas that seeps out of the ground. More arrives from space as cosmic rays, which is why you receive a little extra on a long haul flight. Even your food is slightly radioactive. Bananas, for example, contain potassium, a tiny fraction of which is radioactive, and so does your own body. None of this means you should worry about bananas: the doses are extremely small. In Australia, radiation safety is overseen by ARPANSA, the Australian Radiation Protection and Nuclear Safety Agency, which publishes clear information online."],
    ["Splitting the atom", "Splitting heavy atoms releases enormous energy, which can power cities or, in weapons, destroy them.",
      "In late 1938, the German chemists Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found that bombarding uranium with neutrons produced barium, a much lighter element. Their former colleague Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Frisch worked out the startling explanation: the uranium nucleus had split in two. They called it fission. The pieces weigh slightly less than the original atom, and the missing mass becomes a huge amount of energy, as Einstein's famous E equals mc squared predicts. Each split also frees more neutrons, which can split more atoms in a chain reaction. Controlled, that heat can boil water to drive turbines in a nuclear power station. Uncontrolled, it is the basis of the atomic bomb. Australia holds some of the world's largest known uranium resources, and whether it should have nuclear power remains a lively debate."],
    ["Radiation that heals", "Carefully controlled radiation is one of medicine's most valuable tools for diagnosis and treatment.",
      "The same property that makes radiation dangerous also makes it useful. Because it can damage cells, carefully aimed beams of radiation are used in radiotherapy to destroy cancer cells while sparing as much healthy tissue as possible. Radioactive substances can also act as tracers. A patient might receive a tiny dose of a short lived radioactive substance, and special cameras then track where it goes, revealing how organs such as the heart, bones or thyroid are working. One of the most widely used is technetium-99m, which has a half-life of about six hours. In Australia, it is produced from materials made in the OPAL research reactor at Lucas Heights in Sydney. This is general information only, so talk to your doctor about any specific scan or treatment."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "How was radioactivity discovered?", ["Uranium fogged photographic plates kept in a drawer", "A lightning strike hit a lab", "Marie Curie saw radium glowing at night", "An X-ray machine exploded"], 0, "Becquerel found that uranium darkened wrapped photographic plates without any sunlight."],
    [1, "What distinction did Marie Curie achieve?", ["First woman to fly solo across an ocean", "First person to win two Nobel Prizes", "First person to split the atom", "Inventor of the X-ray machine"], 1, "She won Nobel Prizes in Physics in 1903 and Chemistry in 1911."],
    [2, "Which type of radiation can be stopped by a sheet of paper?", ["Gamma rays", "Beta particles", "Cosmic rays", "Alpha particles"], 3, "Alpha particles are heavy and slow, so paper or the outer layer of skin stops them."],
    [3, "A sample has a half-life of 10 years. How much remains after 20 years?", ["Half", "None", "A quarter", "An eighth"], 2, "Two half-lives leave half of a half, which is a quarter."],
    [5, "Who explained that Hahn and Strassmann had split the uranium nucleus?", ["Marie and Pierre Curie", "Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch", "Ernest Rutherford and Niels Bohr", "Albert Einstein and Max Planck"], 1, "Meitner and Frisch worked out that the uranium nucleus had split, and named the process fission."],
  ]);

WB.L("heat-temperature", "science", "intermediate", "Heat and temperature: what 'hot' really means",
  "Why does a tiled floor feel colder than carpet in the same room? The answer reveals what heat and temperature really are.",
  [
    ["The cold tile mystery", "Two objects at the same temperature can feel different because they move heat away from your skin at different rates.",
      "On a winter morning, step barefoot from the carpet onto the bathroom tiles and the tiles feel icy. Yet if both have been sitting in the same room overnight, a thermometer would show they are at the same temperature. So why the difference? Your skin does not actually sense temperature directly. It senses how quickly heat is flowing into or out of it. Tiles are good conductors of heat, so they pull warmth away from your feet quickly, and your nerves report cold. Carpet is full of trapped air, a poor conductor, so heat leaves your feet slowly and it feels warmer. The same trick explains why metal playground equipment can burn on a summer afternoon while a wooden bench in the same sun feels merely warm to the touch."],
    ["Temperature is jiggling", "Temperature measures how fast particles are moving, while heat is energy flowing from hotter to colder things.",
      "Everything is made of atoms and molecules, and they are never still. In a gas they fly about and collide; in a liquid they slide past one another; in a solid they vibrate in place. Temperature is a measure of how vigorously they are moving, on average. Heat is something slightly different. It is energy that flows from a hotter object to a cooler one because of that temperature difference. When you warm your hands on a mug of tea, the fast moving molecules in the mug knock into the slower ones in your skin and pass on some of their energy. A bathtub of warm water holds far more heat than a cup of boiling water, because it has vastly more molecules, even though the cup is at a higher temperature."],
    ["Absolute zero", "There is a lowest possible temperature, minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, where particle motion is at its minimum.",
      "If temperature is the jiggling of particles, then there must be a point where the jiggling is as small as it can possibly be. That point is called absolute zero: minus 273.15 degrees Celsius. Nothing can be colder. Scientists often use the Kelvin scale, named after the Belfast born physicist William Thomson, Lord Kelvin, which starts at absolute zero. One kelvin is the same size as one degree Celsius, so water freezes at about 273 kelvin and boils at about 373. Absolute zero can never quite be reached, but researchers have cooled atoms to within billionths of a degree of it. At such extreme cold, matter behaves strangely. Some metals lose all electrical resistance, becoming superconductors, and some liquids flow without any friction at all, even creeping up the walls of their containers."],
    ["Three ways heat moves", "Heat travels by conduction through materials, by convection in moving fluids and by radiation through space.",
      "Heat gets from place to place in three ways. Conduction is heat passing directly through a material, particle bumping particle, like the handle of a metal spoon warming up in hot soup. Convection happens in liquids and gases, which can flow. Warm fluid is less dense and rises, cooler fluid sinks, and the circulation carries heat around, which is how a heater warms a room and why the top of a pot of water heats evenly. Radiation needs no material at all. Every warm object gives off infrared light, and that is how the Sun's warmth crosses 150 million kilometres of empty space to reach your face. A vacuum flask blocks all three: a vacuum between its walls stops conduction and convection, and shiny surfaces reflect radiation back where it came from."],
    ["Why water is slow to heat", "Water needs a lot of energy to warm up, which shapes coastal weather and keeps your body cool.",
      "Put a metal saucepan and the water inside it on the stove, and the pan gets hot long before the water does. Water has an unusually high heat capacity, meaning it takes a lot of energy to raise its temperature, and it releases that energy slowly as it cools. This has huge effects on weather. Oceans warm up and cool down far more slowly than land, so coastal cities such as Perth and Sydney tend to have milder swings in temperature than inland towns such as Alice Springs. On a hot afternoon, land heats faster than the sea, warm air rises over the land, and cooler air flows in from the ocean as a sea breeze. Water's thirst for energy also cools you: as sweat evaporates, it carries heat away from your skin."],
    ["Measuring hot and cold", "The Fahrenheit and Celsius scales both date from the 1700s, and Australia switched to Celsius in the early 1970s.",
      "The first reliable thermometers appeared in the early 1700s. The German born instrument maker Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit built accurate mercury thermometers and created the scale that still bears his name, on which water freezes at 32 degrees and boils at 212. A few decades later, the Swedish astronomer Anders Celsius proposed a scale with 100 degrees between freezing and boiling. His original version ran upside down, with water boiling at 0. The scale was flipped soon after his death into the form we use today. Most countries now use Celsius, while the United States still uses Fahrenheit for everyday purposes. Australia switched from Fahrenheit to Celsius in the early 1970s as part of its move to the metric system, which is why older Australians sometimes still talk about a 'century' for a 100 degree day."],
    ["How a fridge moves heat", "A fridge does not make cold: it pumps heat from inside to outside using a fluid that evaporates and condenses.",
      "Heat naturally flows from hot to cold, never the other way on its own. A fridge pushes it uphill, from the cold inside to the warmer kitchen, and it pays for this with electricity. The trick is a special fluid called a refrigerant, circulating in a closed loop of pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant evaporates at low pressure, and evaporating soaks up heat, just as sweat cools your skin. A compressor then squeezes the vapour, making it hot, and it releases that heat through coils at the back or sides of the fridge, which is why they feel warm. The fluid condenses back to a liquid and the cycle repeats. Reverse cycle air conditioners and heat pumps use the same principle, often moving several units of heat for each unit of electricity used."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do tiles feel colder than carpet at the same temperature?", ["Tiles are actually colder", "Tiles conduct heat away from your skin faster", "Carpet produces its own heat", "Tiles absorb moisture from your feet"], 1, "Your skin senses heat flow, and tiles draw heat away much faster than air filled carpet."],
    [2, "What is absolute zero in degrees Celsius?", ["Minus 100", "Minus 459.67", "Zero", "Minus 273.15"], 3, "Absolute zero is minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, or 0 kelvin."],
    [3, "How does the Sun's heat reach Earth?", ["Radiation", "Conduction", "Convection", "Evaporation"], 0, "Radiation needs no material, so infrared and visible light carry the Sun's energy across space."],
    [4, "Why do coastal cities usually have milder temperature swings than inland towns?", ["The sea is always cold", "Coastal air contains less oxygen", "Water warms and cools much more slowly than land", "Sea breezes only happen at night"], 2, "Water's high heat capacity means oceans change temperature slowly, moderating nearby climates."],
    [6, "What does a refrigerator actually do?", ["Creates cold and pumps it in", "Pumps heat from inside to outside", "Removes air from inside", "Freezes the refrigerant permanently"], 1, "A fridge uses an evaporating and condensing fluid to move heat out of the cabinet."],
  ]);

WB.L("enzymes", "science", "advanced", "Enzymes: life's tiny machines",
  "Reactions that would take millions of years on their own happen in your cells in a split second, thanks to molecules called enzymes.",
  [
    ["The problem of slow chemistry", "Most of the chemical reactions life depends on are far too slow to keep anything alive without help.",
      "Your body runs on chemistry. Every second, your cells break down sugars, copy DNA, build proteins and clear away waste. The trouble is that most of these reactions, left to themselves at body temperature, would crawl along at a hopeless pace. Some would take years, and a few would take longer than the age of the Earth. A chemist in a lab might speed things up with strong acids, high pressure or fierce heat, but none of those are options inside a living cell. Life solved the problem with enzymes: large molecules, nearly all of them proteins, that make specific reactions happen enormously faster without being used up themselves. Molecules that speed up reactions in this way are called catalysts, and enzymes are among the most impressive catalysts known."],
    ["From yeast to a crystal", "Enzymes were first studied through fermentation, and in 1926 one was shown to be a protein.",
      "People used enzymes for thousands of years before anyone knew they existed, in brewing beer, baking bread and making cheese. In the 1800s, scientists argued about fermentation. Louis Pasteur believed it needed living yeast cells. In 1897, the German chemist Eduard Buchner showed that juice squeezed from ground up yeast, with no living cells left, could still turn sugar into alcohol. Something in the juice was doing the work, and he won a Nobel Prize for the finding. The word enzyme, coined in 1877, comes from Greek words meaning 'in yeast'. But what were enzymes made of? In 1926, the American chemist James Sumner crystallised an enzyme called urease from jack beans and showed it was a protein. Many doubted him at first, but he too eventually shared a Nobel Prize."],
    ["How enzymes speed things up", "Enzymes lower the energy hurdle a reaction must clear, so many more molecules make it over.",
      "Picture a reaction as a ball that must be pushed over a hill before it can roll down the other side. That hill is called the activation energy. Even if the end products are more stable than the starting materials, few molecules have enough energy to get over the top, so the reaction is slow. An enzyme provides an easier route, effectively lowering the hill. It does this in several ways. It can hold reacting molecules close together in exactly the right orientation, stretch or strain chemical bonds so they break more easily, and position charged parts of itself to stabilise the fleeting, awkward arrangement at the top of the hill. An enzyme does not change where the reaction ends up, only how quickly it gets there, and it emerges unchanged, ready to work again."],
    ["Lock, key and a better fit", "Each enzyme has an active site shaped to grab particular molecules, and it often changes shape as it does.",
      "Enzymes are remarkably choosy. Most work on only one molecule, or a small family of similar ones, called their substrates. The reason is shape. An enzyme is a long chain of amino acids folded into a precise three dimensional form, and somewhere on its surface is a pocket or groove called the active site. In 1894, the German chemist Emil Fischer suggested that a substrate fits its enzyme like a key in a lock. That idea was later refined. In 1958, the American biochemist Daniel Koshland proposed the induced fit model: the active site is somewhat flexible and moulds itself around the substrate as it binds, a bit like a glove tightening around a hand. This shape change can help squeeze the substrate into the form needed for the reaction to happen."],
    ["Heat, acid and the fragile fold", "Enzymes work best within narrow ranges of temperature and acidity, and too much heat destroys their shape.",
      "Because an enzyme's power depends on its precise folded shape, anything that disturbs that shape can stop it working. Warming an enzyme usually speeds it up at first, as molecules move faster and collide more often. Beyond a certain point, though, the heat shakes the fold apart, a process called denaturation, and activity collapses. You see denaturation every time you cook an egg: the clear egg white turns solid and white as its proteins unravel and tangle, and it never turns back. Most human enzymes work best around body temperature. Acidity matters too. Pepsin, which starts digesting protein in your stomach, thrives in strong acid, while enzymes in your small intestine prefer much milder conditions. Some microbes living in hot springs carry enzymes that keep working close to the boiling point of water."],
    ["Turning enzymes on and off", "Cells control their enzymes with switches and inhibitors, and many medicines work by blocking enzymes.",
      "A cell cannot simply leave all its enzymes running at full speed, or it would waste resources and make too much of some products. So enzymes are regulated. Often the end product of a chain of reactions binds to an enzyme near the start of the chain and slows it down, a neat form of feedback that keeps supplies in balance. Other molecules act as inhibitors, either jamming the active site directly or binding elsewhere and changing the enzyme's shape. Medicine makes heavy use of this. Aspirin works partly by blocking enzymes involved in making substances that cause pain and inflammation. Penicillin blocks enzymes bacteria need to build their cell walls. Many other drugs target specific enzymes too. This is general information only, so ask a doctor or pharmacist about any particular medicine."],
    ["Enzymes at work in everyday life", "Enzymes are used in washing powders, food production and research, and scientists now design new ones.",
      "Enzymes are quietly at work in many products you use. Biological laundry powders contain enzymes that break down protein, fat and starch stains, letting them work well in cooler water. Cheesemakers have long used rennet, which contains an enzyme that curdles milk. Enzymes help clarify fruit juice, make sweet syrups from starch and tenderise meat; papaya and pineapple contain enzymes that do this, which is why fresh pineapple can stop jelly from setting. In research, an enzyme from a hot spring bacterium, able to survive repeated heating, made possible the PCR technique used to copy DNA. Scientists are now improving enzymes and even designing new ones. The American chemical engineer Frances Arnold shared the 2018 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for using directed evolution to breed better enzymes in the lab."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a catalyst?", ["A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up", "A product formed at the end of a reaction", "A type of strong acid", "A molecule that stores energy"], 0, "Catalysts, including enzymes, speed reactions and come out unchanged."],
    [1, "What did Eduard Buchner show in 1897?", ["Yeast must be alive to ferment sugar", "Enzymes are made of fat", "Juice from ground up yeast, with no living cells, could still ferment sugar", "Fermentation needs sunlight"], 2, "Buchner showed the active ingredient worked without living cells."],
    [2, "How does an enzyme speed up a reaction?", ["By adding heat to the cell", "By lowering the activation energy", "By changing the final products", "By being consumed in the reaction"], 1, "Enzymes provide an easier route over the energy hill without changing where the reaction ends up."],
    [4, "What happens when an enzyme is denatured?", ["It becomes more active", "It turns into a different enzyme", "It speeds up permanently", "Its folded shape unravels and it stops working"], 3, "Too much heat or the wrong acidity unravels the enzyme's precise fold."],
    [5, "How does penicillin work?", ["It boosts human enzymes", "It blocks enzymes bacteria need to build their cell walls", "It denatures all proteins", "It provides energy to immune cells"], 1, "Penicillin inhibits bacterial enzymes involved in cell wall construction."],
  ]);
WB.L("soil", "science", "intermediate", "The secret life of soil",
  "The ground beneath your feet is not dirt: it is a living, breathing system that took thousands of years to build and feeds almost everything on land.",
  [
    ["A handful of life", "Healthy soil is packed with living things, from bacteria and fungi to worms and insects.",
      "Scoop up a handful of rich garden soil and you are holding one of the most crowded habitats on Earth. Soil scientists often point out that a single teaspoon of healthy soil can contain more microorganisms than there are people on the planet. Many are bacteria, along with fungi, single celled protists and microscopic worms called nematodes. Add mites, springtails, beetle larvae, ants and earthworms, and soil turns out to be teeming with life. Most of these organisms have never been studied, and many have not even been named. That earthy smell after rain comes partly from a compound called geosmin, made by certain soil bacteria. People are remarkably sensitive to it. Far from being lifeless dirt, soil is a busy underground city where the business of recycling life goes on day and night."],
    ["How soil is made", "Soil forms slowly as rock breaks down and mixes with decaying plants and animals.",
      "Soil begins with rock. Over long periods, rock is broken apart by heat and cold, by water freezing in cracks, by plant roots and by chemical reactions with rainwater. Lichens and mosses colonise the surface, and as they live and die they add organic material. Microbes and small animals then break that material down, mixing it with the mineral grains. Gradually, layers form. Near the top is usually a darker layer rich in decomposed organic matter called humus, with paler, more mineral layers below, and the parent rock beneath them all. This process is extremely slow. Depending on climate and rock type, forming just a few centimetres of new soil can take hundreds of years or more. That is why soil lost to erosion is, on any human timescale, effectively lost for good."],
    ["Sand, silt and clay", "The size of mineral particles in soil controls how it holds water, air and nutrients.",
      "The mineral part of soil is made of particles of three main sizes. Sand grains are the largest and feel gritty between your fingers. Silt is finer and feels smooth, a bit like flour. Clay particles are tiny, far too small to see, and feel sticky when wet. The balance of these three gives a soil its texture, and texture matters enormously. Sandy soil drains quickly and warms up fast but struggles to hold water and nutrients. Clay holds plenty of water and nutrients but can become waterlogged, and sets hard when it dries out. Gardeners prize loam, a mix of all three, which holds moisture while still letting air reach the roots. A simple test is to roll a moist pinch between your fingers: clay forms a ribbon, sand falls apart."],
    ["The hidden fungal network", "Most plants partner with soil fungi, trading sugar for water and nutrients.",
      "Dig up the roots of most plants and look closely, and you will often find them wrapped in fine threads of fungus. These partnerships are called mycorrhizae, from Greek words meaning 'fungus' and 'root', and they are thought to involve the large majority of land plant species. The deal is a trade. The plant supplies the fungus with sugars made by photosynthesis. In return, the fungus spreads a vast web of thread-like hyphae through the soil, reaching far beyond the roots and bringing back water and nutrients, especially phosphorus. Fossil evidence suggests these partnerships are ancient and may have helped the first plants colonise land. Popular claims that forests share food and warnings through a fungal 'wood wide web' are debated by scientists, so treat them with caution."],
    ["Earthworms and the recyclers", "Earthworms and decomposers turn dead material back into nutrients that plants can use again.",
      "Charles Darwin was fascinated by earthworms. His last book, published in 1881, was devoted entirely to them and argued that worms had a huge influence on the landscape by constantly churning and enriching the soil. He was right. Earthworms swallow soil and decaying plant matter, then leave behind castings that are rich in nutrients. Their tunnels let air and water reach deep into the ground. Alongside them, bacteria and fungi do the bulk of decomposition, breaking down fallen leaves, dead animals and droppings into simpler substances that plants can absorb again. Without these recyclers, the world would be buried under dead material and nutrients would stay locked away. Australia has its own remarkable worms, including the giant Gippsland earthworm of Victoria, which is commonly around a metre long and lives deep in moist soils."],
    ["Australia's ancient soils", "Many Australian soils are old, weathered and low in nutrients, and native plants have adapted in clever ways.",
      "Compared with many other continents, Australia's soils are often very old and very poor. Much of the land has been geologically stable for a long time, without recent glaciers grinding up fresh rock or widespread volcanoes spreading new mineral rich ash. Over millions of years, rain has leached many nutrients away, and phosphorus in particular is often scarce. Native plants have adapted remarkably. Banksias, grevilleas and their relatives grow dense mats of fine roots, called cluster roots, that release chemicals to free up tightly bound phosphorus. Some native plants are actually harmed by the high phosphorus levels in ordinary garden fertilisers. Australian farmers have long worked with these soils, adding fertilisers such as superphosphate, and scientists continue to study how to farm them sustainably without degrading them further or causing salinity problems."],
    ["Soil, carbon and erosion", "Soil stores huge amounts of carbon, and losing it to erosion has caused some of history's worst disasters.",
      "Soil is one of the planet's great carbon stores. Worldwide, it is estimated to hold more carbon than the atmosphere and all living plants combined, mostly in the form of decaying organic matter. Farming practices that protect and build that organic matter can help keep carbon in the ground and make soil more fertile and better at holding water. When soil is mistreated, the results can be devastating. In the 1930s, drought and poor farming practices on the American Great Plains led to the Dust Bowl, when enormous dust storms stripped away topsoil and forced many families to abandon their farms. Australia has had its own serious erosion and dust storms. The community Landcare movement began in Victoria in the late 1980s and has grown into thousands of local groups working to repair land."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which compound, made by soil bacteria, helps create the smell after rain?", ["Chlorophyll", "Geosmin", "Methane", "Humus"], 1, "Geosmin, produced by certain soil bacteria, is a key part of that earthy smell."],
    [1, "Roughly how long can it take to form a few centimetres of new soil?", ["A few days", "One growing season", "Hundreds of years or more", "Exactly ten years"], 2, "Soil forms extremely slowly, so a few centimetres can take hundreds of years or more."],
    [2, "Which soil type drains quickly but struggles to hold water and nutrients?", ["Sandy soil", "Clay soil", "Loam", "Peat"], 0, "Sand has large particles with big gaps, so water drains through fast."],
    [3, "What do plants give mycorrhizal fungi in exchange for water and nutrients?", ["Oxygen", "Nitrogen gas", "Seeds", "Sugars"], 3, "Plants supply sugars from photosynthesis, and the fungi bring back water and nutrients."],
    [5, "How have banksias adapted to phosphorus poor soils?", ["They grow only in swamps", "They grow cluster roots that free up bound phosphorus", "They get phosphorus from the air", "They eat insects for phosphorus"], 1, "Cluster roots release chemicals that help unlock scarce phosphorus from the soil."],
  ]);
